  ✗ REJECTED: Caller-controlled tokenTransferProxy can receive an unauthor
    Reason: Upon reviewing PSwapLib.megaSwap and ShortLongSpell._deposit and 
_withdraw, I determined this hypothesis is a false positive as stated. 
PSwapLib._approve does approve the supplied spender, but the spender passed by 
the integrating contract is the ShortLongSpell storage variable 
tokenTransferProxy, not a value supplied by the external caller. Both 
openPosition and closePosition pass only augustusSwapper and tokenTransferProxy 
from contract storage into PSwapLib.megaSwap. tokenTransferProxy is assigned 
during initialize(), requires a nonzero address, and the provided code contains 
no public setter allowing users to replace it. Consequently, attacker-controlled
swapData can influence fields such as fromToken and fromAmount, subject to the 
surrounding checks, but cannot control the approval recipient. An incorrectly 
configured deployment or privileged upgrade could select a malicious proxy, 
which is a governance or initialization trust issue rather than an externally 
exploitable access-control flaw. Direct interaction with the library would not 
provide access to the spell's token balances. Additional implementation context 
could be relevant for upgrade authorization, but the claimed caller-controlled 
allowance vulnerability is not demonstrated by this source.
  ? UNCERTAIN: Swap beneficiary is not constrained to the intended recipien
    Note: Upon reviewing PSwapLib.megaSwap in 
blueberry-core/contracts/libraries/Paraswap/PSwapLib.sol and the openPosition 
and closePosition flows in ShortLongSpell.sol, I confirmed that 
MegaSwapSellData.beneficiary is accepted from calldata and forwarded unchanged 
