  ✓ CONFIRMED: ERC20 approval return values are ignored
    Justification: Upon reviewing PSwapLib._approve in 
blueberry-core/contracts/libraries/Paraswap/PSwapLib.sol, specifically the two 
approve calls at lines 14-15, I confirmed that ERC20 return values are ignored. 
The function first invokes inToken.approve(spender, 0) and then 
inToken.approve(spender, amount), but neither result is checked and no SafeERC20
wrapper is used. Consequently, a token that returns false without reverting, or 
otherwise fails to establish the requested allowance, will not cause _approve to
fail. megaSwap then unconditionally invokes 
IParaswap(augustusSwapper).megaSwap(data) at line 25. In the common case, 
Paraswap's subsequent transferFrom will revert because allowance is missing, 
creating a denial-of-service condition for that token; token-specific behavior 
could also produce inconsistent or unsafe outcomes. The shown code contains no 
token allowlist, return-value validation, allowance postcondition check, or 
other guard that mitigates this behavior. The issue is therefore real, although 
practical impact depends on whether such non-compliant tokens can be supplied or
configured.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Unvalidated Base Oracle Address Can Permanently Disable Pric
    Justification: Upon reviewing the UsingBaseOracle constructor and 
IchiVaultOracle.getPrice, I confirmed that the reported availability weakness 
exists. UsingBaseOracle.constructor directly stores the supplied IBaseOracle 
address in the immutable base variable without checking for address(0), deployed
code, or functional oracle behavior. IchiVaultOracle.getPrice subsequently 
invokes base.getPrice(token0) and base.getPrice(token1) after obtaining vault 
data, so a zero address, EOA, or incompatible contract will generally cause the 
external call or ABI decoding to revert for vaults with nonzero supply. Because 
base is immutable, the deployed oracle cannot be repaired or redirected 
afterward. No validation is added by IchiVaultOracle.constructor, which merely 
forwards the argument to the base constructor, and no fallback pricing path 
exists in getPrice. The issue is primarily a deployment or trusted-configuration
error rather than an arbitrary external attacker compromising an existing 
deployment; however, an actor able to deploy or register the oracle can 
permanently create a nonfunctional pricing instance. Constructor checks for 
nonzero address, deployed code, and preferably a valid price response would 
mitigate it.
  ? UNCERTAIN: Unprotected WAuraPools initialization enables configuration 
    Note: Upon reviewing WAuraPools.initialize, mint, and burn, I found that 
initialize is externally callable and protected only by OpenZeppelin's 
initializer modifier, which prevents repeated initialization but does not 
restrict the first caller. It directly stores caller-supplied aura_ and 
auraPools_ addresses without nonzero, code-size, or interface validation, and it
omits __Ownable_init. If an uninitialized proxy is publicly reachable, an 
attacker could initialize it with a malicious AuraPools contract and then cause 
subsequent mint calls to obtain attacker-controlled pool data, transfer users' 
LP tokens into the wrapper, and invoke the malicious deposit path. However, the 
supplied source does not establish that the deployed proxy was left 
uninitialized or that deployment failed to pass initializer calldata atomically.
The missing Ownable initialization is also not independently exploitable here 
because no function in this contract uses onlyOwner. Deployment scripts, proxy 
state, and the referenced interfaces are required to confirm whether the 
conditional takeover scenario exists in practice.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Curve gauge remapping can strand or misroute existing wrappe
    Justification: Upon reviewing WCurveGauge.sol, I confirmed the alleged 
position-remapping logic error. The mint function resolves 
gaugeController.gauges(gid) at mint time, transfers the gauge's lp_token into 
the wrapper, deposits the amount into that gauge, and encodes only gid and 
accCrvPerShares into the ERC1155 token ID. Conversely, burn decodes the same ID 
and independently queries gaugeController.gauges(gid) again before calling 
_mintCrv, withdraw, and lp_token transfer. There is no stored gauge address, 
mapping version, or equality check against the gauge used during minting. If the
controller mapping for gid changes, an existing token can therefore attempt 
withdrawal from the replacement gauge. If the wrapper lacks sufficient balance 
there, redemption reverts and the position is stranded; if it has deposits in 
the replacement gauge, the burn may withdraw and transfer the replacement 
gauge's LP token, misrouting the position. Reentrancy protection and balance 
burning do not address this stale external mapping dependency.
