  ✗ REJECTED: WAuraPools burn can capture rewards accrued for other users
    Reason: Upon reviewing WAuraPools.mint, burn, and pendingRewards, I 
determined that the alleged funds-redirection vulnerability is not demonstrated 
by the provided implementation. Although burn calls 
IAuraRewarder(balRewarder).withdraw(amount, true), the rewarder claim is made 
for the wrapper's aggregate stake, not automatically paid to the burner. The 
subsequently invoked pendingRewards(id, amount) calculates rewards using the 
token ID's encoded reward-per-token checkpoint and the per-token extra-reward 
checkpoints stored in accExtPerShare during mint. It transfers only the amount 
attributable to the burned position; any additional rewards claimed for other 
positions remain in the wrapper for later burns. The same accounting pattern 
exists in WConvexPools. Furthermore, burn invokes _burn(msg.sender, id, amount),
so an approved ERC1155 operator cannot burn another holder's position through 
this function because the owner argument is always msg.sender. The code may have
rounding or design-accounting considerations, but it does not clearly allow a 
small burner to capture aggregate rewards belonging to other users.
  ✗ REJECTED: WIchiFarm burn can redirect rewards accrued for unrelated po
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged funds-redirect vulnerability in 
WIchiFarm.burn and pendingRewards, I determined this is a false positive. 
Although ichiFarm.pendingIchi(pid, address(this)) and harvest(pid, 
address(this)) operate on the wrapper’s aggregate external-farm position, the 
amount transferred to the burner is not the aggregate harvested balance. Each 
ERC1155 token ID embeds the accIchiPerShare value recorded at mint, and 
pendingRewards calculates only the difference between the current pool 
accumulator and that stored snapshot, multiplied by the burned amount. 
WIchiFarm.burn then transfers exactly this position-specific result, while any 
excess ICHI harvested for other positions remains in the wrapper for their later
claims. The _burn(msg.sender, id, amount) check also requires the caller to own 
the specified position and amount. Consequently, minting a minimal position does
not grant access to rewards accrued before its snapshot or to rewards belonging 
to unrelated IDs. The aggregate harvest is therefore an implementation detail, 
not evidence of reward redirection, absent a separate flaw in the external ICHI 
farm’s accumulator accounting.
