protection against this route.
  ✓ CONFIRMED: Externally Mutable Curve Address Provider Can Replace the Tr
    Justification: Upon reviewing the CurveOracle constructor and _getPoolInfo 
function, I confirmed that the oracle trusts dynamically resolved Curve registry
addresses without validating their identity or implementation. Although 
addressProvider is immutable, its get_registry(), get_address(5), and 
get_address(7) results are queried on every price request, so an 
address-provider administrator or a misconfigured deployment can redirect 
resolution to an arbitrary contract. CurveOracle then immediately invokes 
get_pool_from_lp_token, get_n_coins, get_coins, and 
get_virtual_price_from_lp_token on that returned address. The only substantive 
guard is whether the reported pool is nonzero; there is no allowlist, code-hash 
check, expected-provider validation, or verification that the pool and LP token 
correspond. A malicious registry can therefore select attacker-controlled 
underlying tokens and virtual prices, and getPrice subsequently trusts those 
values when calculating LP prices. CoreOracle only checks that the configured 
route returns a nonzero value and does not mitigate this dependency. 
Exploitation requires control or compromise of the provider’s administration, or
an incorrectly configured provider, but the code provides no protection once 
that condition exists.
  ✗ REJECTED: Future oracle timestamps can revert BaseAdapter freshness ch
    Reason: Upon reviewing the alleged future-timestamp availability 
vulnerability in BaseAdapter and the adapter getPrice functions, I determined 
this is a false positive. BaseAdapter itself performs no timestamp arithmetic; 
it only validates configured time gaps. In ChainlinkAdapterOracle.getPrice, the 
freshness condition is `updatedAt < block.timestamp - maxDelayTime`, so a future
`updatedAt` does not participate in subtraction and therefore cannot trigger an 
arithmetic underflow or revert. Instead, the future timestamp would satisfy the 
freshness check and potentially be accepted, representing a possible 
data-integrity concern rather than the claimed availability issue. 
BandAdapterOracle.getPrice uses the same pattern for `lastUpdatedBase` and 
`lastUpdatedQuote`, with no subtraction involving those externally supplied 
timestamps. UniswapV3AdapterOracle.getPrice does not consume an external update 
timestamp or perform the alleged freshness check; it only converts the 
configured gap and queries the Uniswap pool. The only theoretical subtraction 
risk is `block.timestamp - maxDelayTime` if the configured delay exceeds the 
current timestamp, but configured gaps are bounded and this is unrelated to 
future oracle timestamps.
